WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:03.936
Let's turn a busy traffic view into a
snapshot you can replay.

00:00:04.106 --> 00:00:07.506
In this example, we only need requests to
create orders.

00:00:08.052 --> 00:00:12.651
Open Traffic and start with a time range
that includes the requests you want.

00:00:12.822 --> 00:00:16.013
In the Filters bar, set Service to
orders-api.

00:00:16.184 --> 00:00:19.504
If the service is already selected, you can
edit its chip.

00:00:20.052 --> 00:00:21.412
Click the Filters bar.

00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:26.388
Choose Add a filter, select Endpoint, and
enter /api/orders.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:29.073
Check the Reads as summary, then click
Apply.

00:00:29.244 --> 00:00:30.686
Both rows must match.

00:00:31.252 --> 00:00:33.656
The results now show only traffic for this

00:00:33.656 --> 00:00:36.404
service and endpoint within the selected
window.

00:00:36.575 --> 00:00:40.931
Open a POST request from the grid to
confirm you've got the right traffic.

00:00:41.485 --> 00:00:44.387
In the Request tab, inspect the headers or
body.

00:00:44.558 --> 00:00:47.085
Use the Response tab to check what came
back.

00:00:47.256 --> 00:00:52.330
Here the request creates an order, and the
recorded response returned status 201.

00:00:52.885 --> 00:00:54.613
Click Save at the top of Traffic.

00:00:54.784 --> 00:00:58.878
Speedscale creates a snapshot from the
current filters and time range.

00:00:59.049 --> 00:01:01.292
You can find that snapshot later under

00:01:01.292 --> 00:01:04.126
Snapshots and use it as the source for a
replay.

00:01:04.685 --> 00:01:08.350
The next time you need to test this
endpoint, start with that snapshot.
